#![allow(non_upper_case_globals, non_snake_case, non_camel_case_types)]
//! Rust port of `vendor/doomgeneric/info.c` and `info.h`.
//!
//! This module is the giant data table that drives every animated thing
//! in Doom: monsters, projectiles, items, decorations, the player and the
//! player's weapons. It was originally generated by id's `multigen` utility
//! from a higher-level description, which is why the data is hand-written
//! lookup tables rather than runtime code.
//!
//! # Tables exported
//!
//! Three FFI-linked tables are the public face of the module. Together they
//! are referenced from across the simulation (`p_mobj.c`, `p_map.c`,
//! `p_enemy.c`, `p_inter.c`, `p_pspr.c`, ...) and from the renderer
//! (`r_things.c`).
//!
//! * [`sprnames`] - 138 four-character sprite root names plus a `NULL`
//!   sentinel. The renderer scans the WAD directory for lump names whose
//!   first four characters match these tags (e.g. `TROO` matches
//!   `TROOA1`..`TROOR0`) to discover all rotation/frame lumps.
//! * [`states`] - one [`State`] per entry in the `statenum_t` enum
//!   (the [`crate::doom::statenum`] re-export). Each state describes one
//!   animation frame: which sprite to draw, which rotation/frame index,
//!   how many tics to remain, which engine action to run on entry, and
//!   which state to advance to next. States chain via `nextstate` to form
//!   the animation graphs - idle, walk, attack, pain, death, raise - of
//!   every actor in the game.
//! * [`mobjinfo`] - one [`MobjInfo`] per entry in `mobjtype_t`
//!   (`MT_PLAYER` .. `MT_MISC86`). Each entry is a per-thing prototype:
//!   the editor number used in WAD level files, health, radius, height,
//!   mass, damage, speed, the sound effect IDs played in various
//!   situations, the `MF_*` flag bitmask, and a set of `S_*` state indices
//!   that bootstrap the animation graphs (spawnstate, seestate,
//!   missilestate, deathstate, ...).
//!
//! # The state cycle
//!
//! Every actor (`mobj_t`) holds a `state_t*` that the thinker decrements by
//! one tic each frame. When `tics` reaches zero, `P_SetMobjState` advances
//! the actor to `state->nextstate`, runs `state->action` if non-null, and
//! resets the tic counter from the new state. A self-referencing
//! `nextstate == self` with `tics == -1` is the idiomatic "freeze on this
//! frame" terminator (used for `S_NULL`, corpses, dropped items, ...).
//!
//! Each state's `frame` field encodes the sprite rotation/frame index in
//! its low bits and the full-bright bit `FF_FULLBRIGHT == 0x8000` in the
//! high bit. The integer literal `32768` that appears throughout the
//! `states` table is exactly that full-bright flag set on frame `0`; the
//! renderer pulls out the low 15 bits for the lump lookup and uses the top
//! bit to skip light-level shading (muzzle flashes, fireballs, BFG flares).
//!
//! # The action-pointer table
//!
//! `state_t::action` is a function pointer that the simulation invokes
//! when the actor enters that state. In C it is the `actionf_t` union of
//! `void (*)()`, `void (*)(void*)`, and `void (*)(void*, void*)`; in this
//! Rust port we use `Option<unsafe extern "C" fn()>`. The actual targets
//! are nullary in the table (the C ABI is forgiving about extra arguments
//! the callee never reads), and each action function pulls the current
//! actor or player from globals set up by the caller.
//!
//! The action functions themselves are still implemented in C
//! (`p_enemy.c`, `p_pspr.c`, ...) and declared here as an `extern "C"`
//! block. None of them are `pub`; they are only ever invoked indirectly,
//! through `state_t::action`, by the simulation.
//!
//! # Index identifiers
//!
//! The `SPR_*`, `MT_*`, `MF_*` constants in this file are `c_int` aliases
//! for the corresponding C enum values. The `S_*` constants come from
//! [`crate::doom::statenum`] (re-exported via `pub use`) so that this file
//! can write `nextstate: S_PUNCH2` exactly like the C source does. The
//! values *must* match the C enums member-for-member because every entry
//! in `mobjinfo`, `states`, and `sprnames` is initialised by integer index
//! - any drift silently re-targets every cross-table reference. The tests
//! at the bottom of the file (`mt_enum_matches_c_header`,
//! `mf_flags_match_c_header`, `mobjinfo_spawnstate_sprite_cross_reference`,
//! `mobjinfo_respawn_flags_consistent`) lock the indices and flag bits
//! against the original C headers.
//!
//! # FFI / sharing conventions
//!
//! Both [`states`] and [`mobjinfo`] are `#[no_mangle] pub static mut`. The
//! C engine mutates them at runtime in two ways:
//!
//! 1. Dehacked patches (`deh_*.c`) rewrite individual `mobjinfo[]` fields
//!    to alter monster stats.
//! 2. `P_SpawnMobj` initialises new actors by copying from `mobjinfo[]`,
//!    and `P_SetMobjState` reads `states[]` every tic.
//!
//! Concurrent access is therefore the same as in the C codebase: the
//! game thread owns these arrays. The `unsafe impl Sync` blocks below
//! exist solely to satisfy Rust's type system because `*mut c_char`
//! (in `sprnames`) and `Option<unsafe extern "C" fn()>` (in `states`)
//! are not auto-`Sync`. The single-threaded discipline of the engine
//! makes this safe in practice.
//!
//! # Layout invariants
//!
//! [`State`] and [`MobjInfo`] are `#[repr(C)]` with a `const _: () =
//! assert!(...)` size and offset check just below their definitions, so
//! ABI drift against the C structs is caught at compile time.

use std::ffi::{c_char, c_int};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Constants
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Number of entries in [`states`] and in the `statenum_t` enum.
///
/// Must equal the value of `NUMSTATES` (the trailing sentinel of
/// `statenum_t` in `vendor/doomgeneric/info.h`). A mismatch silently
/// truncates or extends every animation graph.
pub const NUMSTATES: usize = 967;

/// Number of entries in [`mobjinfo`] and in the `mobjtype_t` enum.
///
/// Must equal `NUMMOBJTYPES` from `vendor/doomgeneric/info.h`. The test
/// `mt_enum_matches_c_header` enforces this against the C source.
pub const NUMMOBJTYPES: usize = 137;

