/// The full per-thing prototype table: one [`MobjInfo`] per `MT_*`
/// index, in declaration order.
///
/// `P_SpawnMobj(x, y, z, type)` indexes this array by `type` to
/// initialise the fields of a freshly allocated `mobj_t`
/// (health, radius, height, flags, current state, sounds, ...).
/// Throughout the actor's lifetime the simulation reads individual
/// fields back out: `painchance` in `P_DamageMobj`, `seesound` in
/// `A_Look`, `attacksound` in `A_FaceTarget`, `flags & MF_*` in
/// dozens of collision and AI helpers.
///
/// # Cross-references
///
/// * `doomednum` is matched against the `type` field of each WAD
///   thing record (`-1` means the type is never placed in a map).
/// * `spawnstate`, `seestate`, `painstate`, `meleestate`,
///   `missilestate`, `deathstate`, `xdeathstate`, `raisestate` are
///   `S_*` indices into [`states`].
/// * `seesound`, `attacksound`, `painsound`, `deathsound`,
///   `activesound` are [`Sfx`] enum variants resolved by the sound
///   subsystem.
/// * `radius`, `height` are fixed-point ([`FRACUNIT`]).
/// * `flags` is an OR of [`MF_*`](MF_SPECIAL) bits.
///
/// # FFI
///
/// `#[no_mangle] pub static mut` so the C simulation can resolve the
/// symbol `mobjinfo` at link time. Mutability is required because
/// Dehacked patches (`deh_mobj.c`) overwrite individual entries at
/// startup. There is no synchronisation: the engine is single-threaded.
///
/// # Per-entry comments
///
/// Per-entry comments are line comments (`//`) rather than `///` doc
/// comments because Rust does not parse `///` on array element
/// expressions. The `mt_enum_matches_c_header` test below guarantees
/// that the implicit index of each entry equals the corresponding
/// `MT_*` constant.
#[no_mangle]
